data: {"type":"response.output_text.annotation.added","output_index":48,"item_id":"msg_tmp_lyuz8seyc","content_index":0,"annotation_index":15,"annotation":{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://www.verywellhealth.com/sugar-withdrawal-6502380","title":"Sugar Withdrawal: Timeline, Symptoms and Coping Tips","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"The most acute sugar withdrawal symptoms tend to last 2 to 5 days. Remaining symptoms tend to taper off over the next 1 to 4 weeks. Therefore, many people find that the first week after quitting sugar is the hardest, characterized by the most intense symptoms and cravings.6\n\n[...]\n\n## Sugar Withdrawal Timeline\n\n[...]\n\nAfter the initial stage of withdrawal, other symptoms may ensue, such as:6\n\n- Headaches\n- Anxiety\n- Depression\n- Mood swings\n- Decreased concentration\n\n[...]\n\nJust like other types of withdrawal, timelines will vary for each person. The exact timeline you experience may depend on the amount and kinds of sugar you have been eating, your body's metabolism, and your overall diet.12\n\n[...]\n\nEarly symptoms of sugar withdrawal may include:\n\n- Sadness\n- Irritability\n- Tiredness\n- Cravings\n\n[...]\n\n- Problems falling or staying asleep\n- Depressed mood\n- Increased anxiety\n- Irritability\n- Intense cravings for sugary foods\n- Nausea\n- Headaches\n- Fatigue\n\n[...]\n\nWithdrawal symptoms should gradually get better day by day. Eating sugar may reduce your symptoms, but it can also reset the clock on clearing sugar from your body.\n\n[...]\n\nIt can take a few days to a few weeks for the body to adjust to the reduced sugar intake, during which time the withdrawal symptoms will gradually fade away.\n\n[...]\n\n- Reduce sugar intake over time. While some people may be able to go cold turkey, others may benefit from a slower approach. Consider reducing your intake of sugary foods on a week-by-week basis and identifying and eliminating groups of sweetened foods in a staggered fashion.\n- Stay hydrated throughout the day. Be sure to avoid sweetened beverages, which may activate or intensify cravings.\n- Eat small meals throughout the day. Consuming small meals regularly (rather than waiting to eat fewer large meals) may help to keep your blood sugar levels steady and avoid cravings.\n\n[...]\n\n- Add protein and fiber to every meal. Eating foods with protein and fiber can promote feelings of fullness, which can help you manage food cravings.18\n- Engage in physical activity. Exercise has been shown to reduce the intensity of withdrawal symptoms.19\n- Avoid artificial sweeteners. Research says artificial sweeteners may lead to metabolic changes that may increase cravings, food intake, and weight gain.20\n- Get plenty of sleep. Lack of sleep can impair quality of life, intensify withdrawal symptoms, and increase the amount of time it takes to recover.21\n\n[...]\n\nPeople who give up sugar as part of a ketogenic diet may experience more severe sugar withdrawal symptoms. As part of a \"keto\" diet, you eliminate sugar and most carbohydrates from your diet. Carbohydrates are converted into sugar in the body and are your body's preferred energy source.\n\n[...]\n\nEating less sugar is an important step in achieving good health. To prevent diabetes, obesity, and other medical conditions, the Dietary Guidelines for Americans recommends limiting added sugar to 10 grams (g) or less per meal.8\n\n[...]\n\nSymptoms of ketosis may include:10\n\n[...]\n\nTips for managing sugar withdrawal include:17\n\n[...]\n\nRemoving carbs (including sugar) from your diet puts you in a metabolic state called ketosis, where the body burns fat for fuel instead of carbohydrates. This shift comes with an adjustment period that some people say is similar to having the flu.9\n\n[...]\n\nKeto adaption, the process of adapting to ketosis, may take up to three weeks.11 If you are considering a ketogenic diet and are concerned about these symptoms, speak to a registered dietitian knowledgeable about adapting to a keto or low-sugar diet.\n\n[...]\n\n## How to Manage Sugar Withdrawal\n\n[...]\n\n- Bad breath\n- Nausea\n- Fatigue and weakness\n- Diarrhea or constipation\n- Headache\n- Muscle cramps\n\n[...]\n\nResearchers who conduct both animal and human studies have identified withdrawal symptoms, including:67\n\n[...]\n\n- Start each day with a filling, nutritious breakfast, such as steel-cut oatmeal, eggs, and fruit. Continue your day with a healthy, well-balanced diet.\n\n[...]\n\n## Common Symptoms\n\n[...]\n\nWithdrawal occurs when someone eliminates or substantially reduces how much sugar they consume. Before the detox starts, the body becomes accustomed to higher doses of dopamine. Once the source of those high dopamine doses is eliminated, the body asks for more in the form of cravings and other uncomfortable symptoms.\n\n[...]\n\n### Ketosis Symptoms\n\n[...]\n\nIf you are struggling with symptoms of sugar withdrawal and simple home remedies are not providing relief, talk to someone. Withdrawal can be mentally, emotionally, and physically taxing. Don't hesitate to speak to a therapist or trusted friend about what you are going through.\n\n[...]\n\nThe symptoms you experience during sugar withdrawal will likely depend on how quickly you reduce your intake and your body's ability to adapt to the changes.5\n\n[...]\n\nFor instance, since cravings are a common symptom of sugar withdrawal, you may find yourself thinking about sweets or other foods if you cut sugary foods out cold turkey. As is common in addiction, this can lead to binge eating followed by feelings of guilt, anxiety, and shame.13 A cycle of restriction and bingeing can form, and the cycle can be complex to break on your own.\n\n[...]\n\nSugar addiction and withdrawal mirror drug addiction effects. Sugar activates the brain's pleasure centers, releasing dopamine, which makes you feel good and crave more.1\n\n[...]\n\n## When to See a Healthcare Provider\n\n[...]\n\nYou might also find it helpful to speak with a registered dietitian. You may be able to get a referral through your healthcare provider or you can f ind a credentialed professional online who specializes in reducing sugar intake.\n\n[...]\n\nIn-person and online support groups can also be helpful. Some addiction centers have experts who are trained to help people manage the highs and lows of sugar addiction. There are also organizations, like FoodAddicts.org, that provide resources and support groups specifically designed to help you through a food addiction.\n\n[...]\n\nResearchers are investigating the ways that food addiction and disordered eating behaviors are related. In some cases, cutting out sugar abruptly may rebound and lead to binge eating.13\n\n[...]\n\nThe American Psychological Association specifically describes withdrawal as “psychological, behavioral, and cognitive” symptoms that occur when someone stops taking alcohol, cocaine, opioids, or other substances.3"},"sequence_number":1114}

data: {"type":"response.output_text.annotation.added","output_index":48,"item_id":"msg_tmp_lyuz8seyc","content_index":0,"annotation_index":16,"annotation":{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://blog.kissmyketo.com/articles/health/sugar-withdrawal/","title":"Sugar Withdrawal: Causes, Symptoms, and How to Overcome It","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"### Headache\n\n[...]\n\n* Swap out regular sugar forketo-friendly sweetenerslike stevia, monk fruit sweetener, or erythritol.\n* Give up sweet beverages. Have plain water or lemon water during a meal instead,\n\n[...]\n\nConsuming little to no sugar causes your blood glucose levels to drop. We call this decrease in blood glucose “hypoglycemia.” This shift in glucose triggers a general headache. For some people, it can be a migraine6.\n\n[...]\n\nIf your symptoms are overwhelming, consider taking it slow. Instead of cutting out sugar altogether, start with small and doable actions.\n\n[...]\n\n### Cut sugar from your diet gradually\n\n[...]\n\nHeadache is another common side effect of banishing sugar from your diet. At the same time, a person can feel light-headed or nauseated.\n\n[...]\n\nCutting out one bad sugar habit at a time has two benefits:\n\n[...]\n\n### Bad breath\n\n[...]\n\n* Pick one sweet treat to give up. Would it be that afternoon cookie? That breakfast bagel?\n\n[...]\n\n### Intense cravings\n\n[...]\n\n### Low energy\n\n[...]\n\nGoing cold turkey might work for some people, but not everyone.\n\n[...]\n\n*First,*it reduces the intensity of the withdrawal symptoms. Everything becomes more manageable.*Second,*it allows you to make your new healthy habit stick better.\n\n[...]\n\nAre you eliminating sugar as part of your keto diet regimen? Sugar withdrawal on keto can manifest as bad breath. We call thisketo breath.\n\n[...]\n\n### Drink plenty of water\n\n[...]\n\n* Choose a stress-relieving activity that doesn’t involve eating a dessert.\n* The next time you go grocery shopping, check the label for added sugars.\n\n[...]\n\n### Difficulty concentrating\n\n[...]\n\nWithdrawal symptoms don’t last forever. This should motivate you to sustain your efforts. Sugar withdrawal duration usually takes a few days to 2 weeks.\n\n[...]\n\nThe reason for bad breath is the rise in acetone, a ketone body. Acetone exits your body via your urine and breath –thus keto breath.\n\n[...]\n\n### Changes in sleep\n\n[...]\n\n### Eat more protein\n\n[...]\n\n### Anxiety\n\n[...]\n\nIt’s important to manage stress. This takes practice. If you’re living a busy life, learn to slow down. Take a break by meditating, listening to music, or spending time in nature. Make quality sleep a priority as well.\n\n[...]\n\nA drop in your blood glucose levels affects your sleep and its quality. Insomnia can also worsen fatigue, anxiety, and irritability."},"sequence_number":1115}

